```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The eBGP session fails because router2 has the wrong AS number
configured for its peer. Router1 is in AS 1 (confirmed by its
running config: `router bgp 1` and the BGP summary showing
"local AS number 1"). However, router2's neighbor statement reads
`neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 65001` — almost certainly a
fat-finger introduced during the reported configuration change
window (65001 instead of 1).

The mechanism is straightforward BGP OPEN message validation:
every time router1 initiates the TCP session on port 179 and
sends its BGP OPEN with AS=1, router2 compares that value against
its configured `remote-as 65001`, finds a mismatch, and
immediately fires back a NOTIFICATION with error code
"OPEN Message Error / Bad Peer AS" (confirmed by
`show bgp neighbor 193.10.11.1`: "Notifications sent: 4,
Last reset: Notification sent (OPEN Message Error/Bad Peer AS)").
The session never leaves the Idle state, no prefixes are
exchanged, and consequently neither pc1 (195.11.14.2) nor pc2
(200.1.1.2) has a route to the other's network.

The fault is entirely localised to router2, specifically the
single `neighbor` line inside `router bgp 2`.

Root cause: router2's BGP peer statement specifies `remote-as
65001` for neighbor 193.10.11.1, but router1's true AS number
is 1, causing every OPEN attempt to be rejected.

Corrective fix: On router2, replace `neighbor 193.10.11.1
remote-as 65001` with `neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 1` (via
`vtysh -c "conf t" -c "router bgp 2" -c "neighbor 193.10.11.1
remote-as 1"`). The session will establish within one keepalive
interval and both prefixes will propagate.
=== END ===
```